Subject: [PATCH] docs: add literature-backed application examples for entropy
 rate and Kemeny's constant

Both sections already had the theory and a toy weather-chain example,
but no pointer to how these quantities are used in practice. Add one
paragraph to each section referencing an applied paper:

- Entropy rate: Vegetabile et al. (2019, J. Educational and Behavioral
  Statistics) use entropy rate to summarize regularity of estimated
  Markov chains in behavioral time-series studies.
- Kemeny's constant: Levene & Loizou (2002, American Mathematical
  Monthly) give the "random surfer" interpretation of Kemeny's
  constant as a connectivity measure for graphs/networks, which is
  the most commonly cited applied reading of the quantity.

Both references are added to vignettes/markovchainBiblio.bib and
verified to parse cleanly (bibtexparser, 70 entries, 0 failed
blocks) with every \cite/\citep key in the Rmd resolving to a bib
entry.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DYYJYbZa9p9PXV8ZSRhCY4
---
 ...an_introduction_to_markovchain_package.Rmd | 19 +++++++++++++++++
 vignettes/markovchainBiblio.bib               | 21 +++++++++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 40 insertions(+)

diff --git a/vignettes/an_introduction_to_markovchain_package.Rmd b/vignettes/an_introduction_to_markovchain_package.Rmd
index 56c43c9..2106f17 100644
--- a/vignettes/an_introduction_to_markovchain_package.Rmd
+++ b/vignettes/an_introduction_to_markovchain_package.Rmd
@@ -761,6 +761,14 @@ entropyRate(mcWeather, base = exp(1))
 A deterministic chain has entropy rate zero, whereas a chain whose rows are all
 uniform on $n$ states has the maximum value $\log_b(n)$.
 
+Beyond its role as a measure of predictability, the entropy rate has been used
+to compare the regularity of empirically estimated Markov chains across
+subjects or conditions: \cite{vegetabile2019entropy} estimate entropy rates of
+discretized behavioral state sequences (e.g., mother-infant interaction
+coding) to quantify how structured or erratic a process is, showing how the
+same computation implemented in `entropyRate` can serve as a summary
+statistic in applied behavioral and time-series studies.
+
 ## Classification of states
 
 Absorbing states are determined by means of `absorbingStates` method.
@@ -940,6 +948,17 @@ meanFirstPassageTime(mcWeather) %*% piWeather
 The second result has one entry for each possible starting state; apart from
 numerical rounding, all entries equal Kemeny's constant.
 
+Kemeny's constant also has an interpretation as an overall measure of a
+chain's connectivity: a small value indicates that, on average, states are
+reached quickly from one another, while a large value indicates a chain that
+mixes slowly. \cite{leveneLoizou2002} popularized this reading in the context
+of the "random surfer" model of web navigation, relating $K$ to the expected
+number of hyperlink clicks needed to move between pages of a web graph, and
+using it to compare the navigability of different graph topologies. The same
+idea applies to any network representable as a Markov chain (e.g., transport,
+social, or communication networks), where `kemenyConstant` can be used as a
+single-number summary of how well connected the underlying graph is.
+
 ## Mean recurrence time
 
 The `meanRecurrenceTime` method gives the first mean recurrence time (expected number of steps to go back to a state if it was the initial one) for each recurrent state in the transition probabilities matrix for a DTMC. Let's see an example:
